answer.worked:
Differentiate the curve:
$$\begin{align*}
y &= \ln(kx) \\
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{k}{kx} \\
&= \frac{1}{x}
\end{align*}$$
At the point of tangency, the gradient is $m$:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{1}{x}
\end{align*}$$
Since $y=mx$ and $y=\ln(kx)$ intersect at the point of tangency:
$$\begin{align*}
mx &= \ln(kx) \\
\frac{1}{x}\cdot x &= \ln(kx) \\
1 &= \ln(kx) \\
kx &= e \\
x &= \frac{e}{k}
\end{align*}$$
Therefore:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{1}{x} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{e}{k}} \\
&= \frac{k}{e}
\end{align*}$$

